    {
      "kind": "text",
      "text": "Program Format:"
    },
    {
      "kind": "text",
      "text": "Full-time, four-year program. Full-time, advanced standing program with Sheridan College Institute of Technology and Advanced Learning."
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "text": "Admission Information",
      "links": [
        {
          "text": "Admission Information",
          "url": "https://www.torontomu.ca/calendar/2026-2027/programs/fcad/journalism/"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "Full-Time, Four-Year Program"
    },
    {
      "kind": "text",
      "text": "Admission Guidelines: O.S.S.D. with six Grade 12 U/M courses including Grade 12 U English (ENG4U)/Anglais (EAE4U). Notes:"
    },
    {
      "kind": "list_item",
      "text": "A grade of 70 percent or higher will be required in ENG4U/EAE4U."
    },
    {
      "kind": "list_item",
      "text": "Subject to competition, candidates may be required to present averages/grades above the minimum."
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "Advanced Standing for Graduates of the Journalism Diploma Program from Sheridan College Institute of Technology and Advanced Learning"
    },
    {
      "kind": "text",
      "text": "Toronto Metropolitan University (TMU) will consider graduates of the following Sheridan College two-year diploma programs for advanced-level admission to TMU’s Journalism Program:"
    },
    {
      "kind": "list_item",
      "text": "Sheridan College Journalism"
    },
    {
      "kind": "list_item",
      "text": "Sheridan College Journalism – Broadcast Diploma (former program)"
    },
    {
      "kind": "list_item",
      "text": "Sheridan College Journalism – Print Diploma (former program)"
    },
    {
      "kind": "text",
      "text": "Projected completion of the Journalism degree requirements is five semesters and is dependent on a number of variables, including diploma program studies completed, space availability and the individual student’s progress in the program curriculum."
    },
    {
      "kind": "text",
      "text": "Admission Guidelines:"
    },
    {
      "kind": "list_item",
      "text": "O.S.S.D. with six Grade 12 U/M courses including Grade 12 U English (ENG4U)/Anglais (EAE4U)."
    },
    {
      "kind": "list_item",
      "text": "A grade of 70 percent or higher is required in ENG4U/EAE4U."
    },
    {
      "kind": "list_item",
      "text": "Completion of an acceptable Sheridan College two-year diploma program with a cumulative grade point average of 3.0 (B) is required."
    },
    {
      "kind": "list_item",
      "text": "Subject to competition, candidates may be required to present averages/grades above the minimum."
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "text": "Program Overview/Curriculum Information",
      "links": [
        {
          "text": "Program Overview/Curriculum Information",
          "url": "https://www.torontomu.ca/calendar/2026-2027/programs/fcad/journalism/"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "text",
      "text": "Students are introduced to journalism as practised across all forms of media within the first two years, after which they may choose courses to concentrate in specific media (online, newspaper, magazine and broadcast) or gain expertise in a cross-media skill such as editing, or in a specific beat in journalism (including sports, business or international reporting)."
    },
    {
      "kind": "text",
      "text": "After taking courses building skills and evaluating theories associated with journalism, students may finish their program with an internship at a professional news organization and/or with the chance to work on the School of Journalism's print, broadcast and online products (see the School's website for details)."
    },
    {
      "kind": "text",
      "text": "Students are coached and assisted by instructors drawn from major newspapers, magazines, online news, and television and radio networks. Students also take a variety of liberal studies and professionally-related subjects."
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "Liberal Studies"
    },
    {
      "kind": "text",
      "text": "Students must take two lower level liberal studies courses and four upper level liberal studies courses to graduate. Students must not choose courses that are restricted for their program or major.",
      "links": [
        {
          "text": "lower level",
          "url": "https://www.torontomu.ca/calendar/2026-2027/liberal-studies/table_a/"
        },
        {
          "text": "upper level",
          "url": "https://www.torontomu.ca/calendar/2026-2027/liberal-studies/table_b/"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "text",
      "text": "Please refer to the liberal studies chapter of this calendar for more information on the Liberal Studies Policy. Further information on liberal studies can also be found at the Faculty of Arts' Liberal Studies website (opens in new window) .",
      "links": [
        {
          "text": "liberal studies",
          "url": "https://www.torontomu.ca/calendar/2026-2027/liberal-studies/"
        },
        {
          "text": "Liberal Studies website (opens in new window)",
          "url": "https://www.torontomu.ca/content/ryerson/liberal-studies/"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "text",
      "text": "Table A - Lower Level Restrictions"
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "Minors"
    },
    {
      "kind": "text",
      "text": "Students may pursue any Minor offered by Toronto Met (with some exceptions). Please refer to the Minors chapter of this calendar for further information on individual Minor requirements and exclusions.",
      "links": [
        {
          "text": "Minors",
          "url": "https://www.torontomu.ca/calendar/2026-2027/minors/"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
